morgan: Quick UX/A11y nit: when a deep-link includes a fragment/hash to an in-page anchor (e.g. /assets/models/123#overview) and we also resolve an alias from an old path, what's the single source of truth for where the page should land? We had reports users landed at top of page after the IA move.

tara: Short answer: preserve the fragment and attempt to scroll to it after canonicalization/expansion. Show canonical breadcrumb and a small moved label. Also surface a skip-link target so keyboard users can jump to the anchor quickly.

devin: Implementation order matters. Router should: 1) resolve alias -> canonical route 2) render/expand nav 3) wait for anchor node to exist -> then scrollIntoView. Rough pseudo: 
```
const onRoute = async (path, hash) => {
  const resolved = resolveAlias(path);
  renderRoute(resolved);
  await waitFor(() => document.querySelector(hash));
  document.querySelector(hash).scrollIntoView({behavior: 'smooth'});
};
```
This avoids landing at top then jumping later.

alex: UX: also show a subtle microcopy in the header for anchors that were part of the old grouping: "Jumped to Overview (moved from Workspaces)" with an inline "Why" link. Keep it non-modal and low-contrast.

sam: A11y: important bits: when we auto-scroll to a hash target, we must also move keyboard focus to a logical focusable element (preferably the section heading or a skip-link). Use aria-live polite to announce "Section: Overview  moved to Assets > Models" so screen readers get context. Avoid focus traps.

morgan: Edge case: if anchor target is inside a collapsed panel (e.g. model details collapsed by default), expand parent first then scroll. Otherwise user won't find it.

devin: Agree. We'll add a small retry/backoff for waiting on the element in case render is async. Also add a metric: percent of anchor-resolves that required aliasing vs direct.

tara: Copy for skip-link: "Skip to section: Overview (moved)". For the moved label accessible name include original path info.

alex: Keyboard shortcut note: our global "g m" shortcut should land at canonical Models index if used, but if the user supplies a fragment we should respect it and land in the section. Document this in shortcuts help.

